course: everyday
lesson: 381
title: Girls who fight and double standards
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At break Maddie and Jake both got into the same scuffle over the football. The dinner lady told Jake off and kept him in, but to Maddie she only sighed: «I expected better from a girl.» Jake was furious — «same shove, why's she let off?» — and Maddie was cross too, because «better from a girl» made her feel like she wasn't a proper part of the game at all. Both of them were right that something was unfair.
explain: The street was just as muddled. When Maddie fought, kids said «Maddie's so cool»; when Jake did exactly the same, they said «that's horrible». After Connor punched Maddie's nose, half the class said «Connor's a coward, he hit a girl» and the other half said «Maddie asked for it, she shoves boys all day». Kieran told Maddie she couldn't come to a fight because she was a girl — and the very next day begged her to come because she was «the best fighter». When Kerry punched a boy in the arm for teasing her, everyone said «good for her», though a boy doing the same would have been in trouble. And Jake's dad told him «never hit a girl» but «hit back if a boy hits you».
explain: Sam got teased for not fighting, while Lily was praised for exactly the same thing. A teacher even told Lily to «act more like Maddie and stand up for herself», as if standing up meant using fists. When a Station Road girl slapped Lily, some boys just laughed «girl fight!» — as if a girl's slap didn't hurt or didn't count.
explain: All these rules have one thing in common: they judge the person, not the act. A fair rule is the same for everyone. Hurting someone is wrong whether a boy or a girl does it and whether a boy or a girl is hurt. Choosing not to fight is just as brave for Sam as it is for Lily, and standing up for yourself can mean saying no, walking off and telling an adult — for anyone.
fact: A double standard is when the same action is judged differently depending on who does it.
fact: Hitting someone is wrong whether the person who hits is a boy or a girl.
fact: A punch hurts just as much whoever throws it and whoever gets it.
fact: «They asked for it» never makes hitting someone right; annoying behaviour should be reported, not punished with fists.
fact: Choosing not to fight takes courage for boys as well as girls.
fact: Standing up for yourself can mean saying a firm «stop», walking away and telling a trusted adult.
fact: Laughing at a fight because it is «a girl fight» treats real pain as a show.
fact: Adults sometimes say things that do not fit together; it is fine to ask them politely what they really mean.
fact: Teachers and dinner staff are meant to apply school rules to everyone in the same way; if you think a rule was applied unfairly, you can say so calmly later.
fact: Being admired for fighting can trap a person into fighting again and again to keep the name.
exercise: The dinner lady told Jake off but said to Maddie «I expected better from a girl». Why was that a double standard? | The same shove was judged differently because of who did it | Girls are never allowed to play football at break time | Jake had done something much worse than a shove | Maddie had been the one who was hurt
exercise: Why did Maddie feel cross even though she was let off? | It made her feel she did not truly count as one of the players | She actually wanted to be kept in at break with Jake | She thought Jake should be let off too | She did not like the dinner lady's loud, cross voice at all
exercise: Kids called Maddie «cool» for fighting and Jake «horrible» for the same thing. What is the fair view? | The fighting was the same, so it should be judged the same | Maddie's fighting is fine because it is surprising for a girl | Jake's fighting is worse because boys are stronger | Neither of them did anything wrong at all
exercise: After Connor punched Maddie's nose, some said «she asked for it». What is true? | Punching is never right | Shoving boys means she should expect a punch | It is fine to punch if you are provoked often | It only counts if it happens in front of a teacher
exercise: What should Connor have done if Maddie kept shoving him? | Told her to stop and told a teacher | Shoved her back much harder than she shoved | Waited and punched her later | Got all his friends to shove her too
exercise: Kieran said «you can't come, you're a girl» and then «we need you, you're the best fighter». What does this show? | He used whatever rule suited him that day | He had honestly changed his mind about fighting | He really wanted to keep Maddie safe from harm | Girls are allowed in fights only on some days
exercise: Jake's dad said «never hit a girl» but «hit back if a boy hits you». What rule would be fairer and safer? | Don't hit anyone; get away and get help | Hit girls back, but only if they hit you first | Hit everyone back, whether it's a boy or a girl | Only hit back boys who are bigger than you
exercise: Sam was teased for not fighting and Lily was praised for it. What is the fair view? | Not fighting is sensible and brave for both | Not fighting is only right for girls, not for boys | Sam should fight so nobody teases him | Lily should fight back to be tough like Maddie
exercise: The teacher told Lily to «act more like Maddie». What would really help Lily stand up for herself? | Learning to say a firm «stop», walk off and tell an adult | Learning to punch back properly, like Maddie does at break | Never speaking to anyone at break | Waiting until she is older to say anything
exercise: Kerry punched a boy in the arm for teasing and everyone said «good for her». What is true? | Teasing is wrong, but punching was not the right answer | It was fine, because a girl punching a boy doesn't count | It was fine because he deserved it | It was fine because it was only his arm she hit
exercise: When a girl slapped Lily, some boys laughed «girl fight!». Why was that unkind? | They treated Lily's real hurt as a show | They should have cheered for Lily instead of laughing | Girls' slaps are not real fights | They should have joined in the fight
exercise: What could a bystander do when Lily was slapped? | Check she is OK and get an adult | Film it so you can show everyone later | Shout «fight! fight!» to get a crowd | Tell the girl to slap harder next time
exercise: Which of these is a double standard? | «It's cool when she does it, gross when he does» | «Nobody hits anyone at this school, ever» | «Both of you can stay in at break» | «Hurting people is wrong for everyone, boy or girl»
exercise: Why can being called «so cool» for fighting be bad for Maddie? | She may feel she has to keep fighting to keep the name | It means she will never get hurt by anyone in a fight | It means teachers will let her off | It makes her the fastest runner
exercise: How could Jake politely point out the unfair telling-off later? | «Miss, we both shoved, so it should be the same for both» | «You always pick on boys, it's sexist and I hate this school» | «Then let me off too or I'll do it again» | «I'm going to tell my dad on you»
exercise: Jake asks his dad «so can I hit a girl if she hits me?». What answer fits the fair rule? | No — don't hit anyone; get away and tell an adult | Yes, but only once, so she knows not to | Yes, if she is bigger than you are | No, but you can go and hit her brother instead
test: playground: «oi Maddie's gonna batter Connor, she's well hard, it's sick». What's the wise reply? | Nah, someone's gonna get hurt — get a teacher | Yeah, film it and put it in the chat | Only if Connor goes first, then I'm in | Bet you a quid she wins it easy, she's well hard
test: «he hit a girl, he's dead at home time» says Connor's mate's cousin. What's true? | Revenge fighting just makes more fights; tell an adult | That's fair, he deserves it after what he did to her | It's fine because he's a boy | Only if it's outside school
test: «she's a girl, she can't really hurt you, let her hit you lol». True? | No, anyone's punch can really hurt | Yes, girls can't really hurt anyone | Yes, if she's younger and smaller than you | Only if she's wearing big rings on her hand
test: Teacher: «Who started it?» Jake: «we both did, miss». Maddie stays quiet. What's fair for Maddie to do? | Say yes, she shoved too | Say only Jake started it | Say she was just watching | Cry so she gets let off by Miss
test: «Sam's a wimp, won't even fight, Lily's sweet cos she won't either». What's wrong with that? | It judges the same choice differently by who makes it | Nothing, that's just how it is and always will be | Sam should just fight then | Lily is a wimp too then, if Sam is
test: Lily's mate: «just slap her back, Lily, show her!». What's the wiser move? | Walk off and tell a teacher what happened | Slap her back harder than she slapped you | Pull her hair instead, it's less obvious | Wait and do it after school
test: Kieran, next day: «we need u Maddie ur the best fighter». What's a good answer? | No — and I'm not doing street fights at all | OK, but only if you pay me first | OK, but only against the girls from their street | Only if Jake comes as well
test: «Maddie asked for it, she shoves everyone, so the punch was fine». Is it? | No, shoving should be reported, not punished with a punch | Yes, because she was the one who started it | Yes, if it was only her nose that got hit | Only if she shoved him first that same day in the line
test: Gran: «Nice girls don't fight, nice boys hit back.» What's a fairer saying? | Nice people don't hurt anyone | Only nice boys fight | Nobody should ever stand up for themselves | Girls must always hit back too
test: Group chat: «GIRL FIGHT at the gates 3:15 come watch». Best move? | Don't go; tell a teacher before 3:15 | Go but stand at the very back of the crowd | Go and film it for the chat | Go so you can tell everyone who won
test: Jake: «why does Maddie get let off, it's not fair». What can he do that actually helps? | Calmly ask the teacher later to treat them both the same | Shove Maddie again to make the point clear | Refuse to go to dinner | Tell everyone the dinner lady hates all the boys in the school
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a double standard in respect of physical aggression? | The identical act is judged differently according to the sex of the person committing it | The act is judged solely according to the harm it causes to the other | All parties are subject to the same school sanction without exception | Aggression is condemned irrespective of the person concerned
exam: It is given that a pupil was repeatedly shoved and subsequently struck the other pupil. Identify the statement consistent with established principles. | Provocation does not render striking another person acceptable | Repeated provocation renders retaliation acceptable | Striking is acceptable if directed at a pupil of the opposite sex | Striking is acceptable if no adult is present
exam: Select the response most consistent with a single fair rule concerning blows between children. | No child should strike another, irrespective of sex | Boys may strike boys but may never strike girls | Girls may strike boys but not girls | Retaliation is required whenever one is struck by another child
exam: Identify the principal reason why declining to fight is properly regarded as courageous in both boys and girls. | It requires one to bear teasing and pressure without causing harm | It ensures that one will never again be teased by one's peers | It is required only of persons of a particular sex | It guarantees the lasting approval of all one's peers and teachers
